1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,111
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is Intro to Computer

2
00:00:04,111 --> 00:00:08,622
Science, Unit 6: Security and Society.
Let's learn how to stay safe online, and

3
00:00:08,622 --> 00:00:13,076
how computing affects all of us. After
every lesson, we'll work through a full

4
00:00:13,076 --> 00:00:17,301
example step by step, and we'll finish by
putting the whole unit together.

5
00:00:18,475 --> 00:00:23,275
Every time you log in, a computer has to
answer one question: are you really you?

6
00:00:23,725 --> 00:00:25,218
That's called authentication.

7
00:00:26,368 --> 00:00:30,908
There are three kinds of proof. Something
you know, like a password. Something you

8
00:00:30,908 --> 00:00:35,115
have, like your phone. And something you
are, like your fingerprint or face.

9
00:00:36,265 --> 00:00:40,788
Attackers guess passwords with programs
that try millions of guesses a second.

10
00:00:41,238 --> 00:00:45,991
A short password with only lowercase
letters can be cracked in seconds. Each

11
00:00:45,991 --> 00:00:49,494
extra character multiplies the number of
guesses needed.

12
00:00:50,644 --> 00:00:55,846
So make passwords long. A passphrase of
four random words is long, and easy to

13
00:00:55,846 --> 00:00:56,446
remember.

14
00:00:56,896 --> 00:01:01,843
And never reuse a password. If one site
leaks it, attackers will try it everywhere

15
00:01:01,843 --> 00:01:02,144
else.

16
00:01:03,294 --> 00:01:08,084
Two-factor authentication adds a second
kind of proof, usually a code on your

17
00:01:08,084 --> 00:01:08,457
phone.

18
00:01:08,907 --> 00:01:13,110
Even if someone steals your password, they
still can't get in without your phone.

19
00:01:14,260 --> 00:01:18,856
Good websites never store your actual
password. They store a hash, a scrambled

20
00:01:18,856 --> 00:01:21,449
fingerprint of it that can't be turned
back.

21
00:01:21,899 --> 00:01:26,902
A password manager can create and remember
a strong, different password for every

22
00:01:26,902 --> 00:01:27,211
site.

23
00:01:28,361 --> 00:01:33,289
Your turn. Which is stronger: P at sign ss
one, or purple tiger river lamp?

24
00:01:33,739 --> 00:01:34,934
Pause the video now.

25
00:01:35,384 --> 00:01:40,051
purple tiger river lamp. It's much longer,
so there are far more possibilities to

26
00:01:40,051 --> 00:01:40,397
guess.

27
00:01:40,847 --> 00:01:43,834
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

28
00:01:44,983 --> 00:01:48,823
Let's do the math an attacker does, and
see why length beats everything.

29
00:01:49,973 --> 00:01:54,154
The problem: an attacker's program tries
ten billion guesses per second.

30
00:01:54,604 --> 00:01:58,871
How long does it take to try every
possible password, for four different

31
00:01:58,871 --> 00:01:59,938
kinds of password?

32
00:02:01,088 --> 00:02:05,420
Count the possible passwords: the number
of characters to choose from, raised to

33
00:02:05,420 --> 00:02:06,016
the length.

34
00:02:06,466 --> 00:02:11,728
Lowercase has twenty six choices. Mixed,
with upper, lower and digits, has sixty

35
00:02:11,728 --> 00:02:11,991
two.

36
00:02:12,441 --> 00:02:15,065
Then divide by ten billion guesses per
second.

37
00:02:16,215 --> 00:02:21,229
Eight lowercase letters: about two hundred
billion options. Cracked in about twenty

38
00:02:21,229 --> 00:02:21,954
one seconds.

39
00:02:22,404 --> 00:02:24,644
Eight mixed characters: about six hours.

40
00:02:25,094 --> 00:02:27,824
Twelve mixed characters: about ten
thousand years.

41
00:02:28,274 --> 00:02:33,253
Sixteen lowercase letters: about one
hundred forty thousand years. Longer beats

42
00:02:33,253 --> 00:02:33,757
fancier.

43
00:02:34,907 --> 00:02:40,480
Each extra character multiplies the work.
A long passphrase is both strong and easy

44
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:41,286
to remember.

45
00:02:41,736 --> 00:02:47,146
But no password is safe if it's reused and
leaked. Use a different one for each site,

46
00:02:47,146 --> 00:02:49,565
and turn on two factor authentication.

47
00:02:50,715 --> 00:02:55,824
Your turn. A four digit PIN has ten
choices per digit. How many PINs are

48
00:02:55,824 --> 00:03:00,294
possible, and how long would ten billion
guesses a second take?

49
00:03:00,744 --> 00:03:01,938
Pause the video now.

50
00:03:02,388 --> 00:03:07,279
Ten thousand PINs. That's less than a
millionth of a second. That's why phones

51
00:03:07,279 --> 00:03:09,599
lock you out after a few wrong tries.

52
00:03:10,049 --> 00:03:11,137
Next, encryption.

53
00:03:12,287 --> 00:03:16,679
When you send a message across the
internet, it passes through many computers

54
00:03:16,679 --> 00:03:17,706
you don't control.

55
00:03:18,156 --> 00:03:20,374
Encryption keeps it secret along the way.

56
00:03:21,524 --> 00:03:26,410
Encryption turns readable plaintext into
scrambled ciphertext, using a key.

57
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:30,700
Only someone with the right key can
decrypt it back into plaintext.

58
00:03:31,850 --> 00:03:36,587
One of the oldest examples is the Caesar
cipher. Shift every letter by a fixed

59
00:03:36,587 --> 00:03:37,012
number.

60
00:03:37,462 --> 00:03:43,286
With a shift of three, A becomes D, B
becomes E, and CAT becomes F D W.

61
00:03:44,436 --> 00:03:49,588
The Caesar cipher is easy to break. There
are only twenty five possible shifts, so a

62
00:03:49,588 --> 00:03:51,796
computer can try them all instantly.

63
00:03:52,246 --> 00:03:57,216
Modern encryption uses keys so large that
trying every one would take longer than

64
00:03:57,216 --> 00:03:58,689
the age of the universe.

65
00:03:59,839 --> 00:04:04,655
In symmetric encryption, the same key
locks and unlocks. It's fast, but both

66
00:04:04,655 --> 00:04:06,303
people need the key first.

67
00:04:06,753 --> 00:04:12,061
In public key encryption, each person has
two keys: a public key anyone can use to

68
00:04:12,061 --> 00:04:16,268
lock a message, and a private key only
they can use to unlock it.

69
00:04:17,418 --> 00:04:23,405
Public key encryption is what makes HTTPS
work. Your browser and the server use it

70
00:04:23,405 --> 00:04:27,786
to agree on a secret key, safely, even
over an open network.

71
00:04:28,936 --> 00:04:32,840
Your turn. Decrypt this Caesar cipher with
a shift of three: K L.

72
00:04:33,290 --> 00:04:34,484
Pause the video now.

73
00:04:34,934 --> 00:04:40,268
Shift each letter back by three. K becomes
H, and L becomes I. It says HI.

74
00:04:40,718 --> 00:04:43,704
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

75
00:04:44,855 --> 00:04:49,553
Let's encrypt and decrypt a word with a
Caesar cipher, including letters that wrap

76
00:04:49,553 --> 00:04:49,954
around.

77
00:04:51,104 --> 00:04:56,800
The problem: encrypt the word PIZZA with a
shift of three. Then decrypt K H O O R.

78
00:04:57,950 --> 00:05:01,598
To encrypt, move each letter three places
forward in the alphabet.

79
00:05:02,048 --> 00:05:07,466
After Z, wrap around to A. So X becomes A,
Y becomes B, and Z becomes C.

80
00:05:07,916 --> 00:05:09,730
To decrypt, move three places back.

81
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:12,821
P moves to S. I moves to L.

82
00:05:13,271 --> 00:05:18,391
Z wraps around: Z, A, B, C. So both Zs
become C. A moves to D.

83
00:05:18,841 --> 00:05:25,028
PIZZA becomes S L C C D. Decrypting K H O
O R, three back each, gives HELLO.

84
00:05:26,178 --> 00:05:31,490
PIZZA encrypts to S L C C D, and K H O O R
decrypts to HELLO.

85
00:05:31,940 --> 00:05:37,217
The weakness: there are only twenty five
possible shifts. A computer tries them all

86
00:05:37,217 --> 00:05:41,732
instantly. Modern keys have more options
than there are atoms on Earth.

87
00:05:42,882 --> 00:05:45,826
Your turn. Decrypt M B Q, with a shift of
one.

88
00:05:46,276 --> 00:05:47,470
Pause the video now.

89
00:05:47,920 --> 00:05:51,056
Move each letter back one: L A P. The word
is LAP.

90
00:05:51,506 --> 00:05:53,426
Next, threats like phishing and malware.

91
00:05:54,576 --> 00:05:58,117
Most attacks don't break encryption. They
trick people instead.

92
00:05:58,567 --> 00:05:59,954
Let's learn how to spot them.

93
00:06:01,104 --> 00:06:05,803
Phishing is a fake message that pretends
to come from someone you trust, like your

94
00:06:05,803 --> 00:06:06,949
bank or your school.

95
00:06:07,399 --> 00:06:11,645
It tries to get you to click a link, or
type your password into a fake page.

96
00:06:12,795 --> 00:06:17,507
Look for warning signs. A sender address
that's slightly wrong. Urgent pressure,

97
00:06:17,507 --> 00:06:22,160
like your account will be closed today.
And links that go somewhere unexpected.

98
00:06:22,610 --> 00:06:26,023
Before you click, hover over a link to see
where it really goes.

99
00:06:27,173 --> 00:06:29,264
Malware is software made to cause harm.

100
00:06:29,714 --> 00:06:34,693
A virus attaches to other files.
Ransomware locks your files and demands

101
00:06:34,693 --> 00:06:37,735
money. Spyware secretly watches what you
do.

102
00:06:38,885 --> 00:06:43,855
Malware usually gets in through downloads,
attachments, or old software with known

103
00:06:43,855 --> 00:06:44,219
holes.

104
00:06:44,669 --> 00:06:49,447
So only download from trusted sources, and
keep your system and apps updated.

105
00:06:50,597 --> 00:06:55,148
Protect yourself with a few habits:
updates on, strong unique passwords,

106
00:06:55,148 --> 00:06:59,131
two-factor authentication, and backups of
your important files.

107
00:06:59,581 --> 00:07:04,018
If a message feels off, contact the
company directly, not through the message.

108
00:07:05,168 --> 00:07:10,289
Your turn. An email says: your account
will be deleted in one hour, click here to

109
00:07:10,289 --> 00:07:15,536
log in. The link goes to secure dash login
dot example dot net. What should you do?

110
00:07:15,986 --> 00:07:17,181
Pause the video now.

111
00:07:17,631 --> 00:07:22,178
Don't click it. The urgency and the
strange link are signs of phishing. Go to

112
00:07:22,178 --> 00:07:24,009
the real site yourself instead.

113
00:07:24,459 --> 00:07:27,446
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

114
00:07:28,596 --> 00:07:32,905
Let's inspect a suspicious email, clue by
clue, and decide what to do.

115
00:07:34,055 --> 00:07:37,469
The problem: you get this email. Is it
real, or phishing?

116
00:07:37,919 --> 00:07:43,448
From: support at schoo1 portal dot com.
Subject: Your account closes today! Click

117
00:07:43,448 --> 00:07:45,428
here to verify your password.

118
00:07:46,578 --> 00:07:50,333
Check four things: the sender, the
pressure, the link, and what it asks for.

119
00:07:50,783 --> 00:07:54,175
If even one of them looks wrong, stop, and
don't click anything.

120
00:07:55,325 --> 00:07:59,826
The sender: school is spelled with the
number one instead of an L. Red flag.

121
00:08:00,276 --> 00:08:02,452
The pressure: closes today. Red flag.

122
00:08:02,902 --> 00:08:07,318
The link: hovering shows a strange web
address, not your school's. Red flag.

123
00:08:07,768 --> 00:08:12,717
The request: your password. Real schools
never ask for it by email. Red flag.

124
00:08:13,867 --> 00:08:18,284
Four red flags. It's phishing. Don't
click, don't reply. Report it, and delete

125
00:08:18,284 --> 00:08:18,454
it.

126
00:08:18,904 --> 00:08:23,288
If you're unsure, go to the real site
yourself by typing its address, never

127
00:08:23,288 --> 00:08:24,749
through the email's link.

128
00:08:25,899 --> 00:08:30,894
Your turn. A text says you won a free
phone, and asks you to pay two dollars for

129
00:08:30,894 --> 00:08:32,705
shipping. Name two red flags.

130
00:08:33,155 --> 00:08:34,349
Pause the video now.

131
00:08:34,799 --> 00:08:38,895
It's too good to be true, and it asks for
payment details. It's a scam.

132
00:08:39,345 --> 00:08:41,308
Next, privacy, AI and ethics.

133
00:08:42,458 --> 00:08:47,828
Computers can do amazing things. But they
also raise big questions about fairness

134
00:08:47,828 --> 00:08:48,623
and privacy.

135
00:08:49,073 --> 00:08:50,225
Let's look at a few.

136
00:08:51,375 --> 00:08:56,236
Apps and websites collect data about you:
what you click, where you go, who you talk

137
00:08:56,236 --> 00:08:56,410
to.

138
00:08:56,860 --> 00:09:00,999
Even small pieces, put together, can
reveal a lot about a person.

139
00:09:02,149 --> 00:09:07,818
Personally identifiable information, or
PII, is data that can identify you, like

140
00:09:07,818 --> 00:09:10,511
your name, address, birthday, or face.

141
00:09:10,961 --> 00:09:15,431
Share as little of it as you can, and
check the privacy settings of the apps you

142
00:09:15,431 --> 00:09:15,655
use.

143
00:09:16,805 --> 00:09:21,605
Many A I systems learn from examples
instead of following rules a person wrote.

144
00:09:22,055 --> 00:09:27,045
A spam filter, for example, studies
thousands of emails labeled spam or not

145
00:09:27,045 --> 00:09:28,775
spam, and learns patterns.

146
00:09:29,925 --> 00:09:33,978
That's powerful, but it means the A I
learns whatever is in its data.

147
00:09:34,428 --> 00:09:39,447
If the training data is unfair or
incomplete, the A I can be unfair too.

148
00:09:39,447 --> 00:09:44,954
This is called bias. A face recognition
system trained mostly on some faces may

149
00:09:44,954 --> 00:09:46,417
work worse on others.

150
00:09:47,567 --> 00:09:52,700
People who build these systems can reduce
bias by using diverse data, testing on

151
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:56,485
many groups, and letting humans review
important decisions.

152
00:09:56,935 --> 00:10:00,625
As a computer scientist, you get to help
make technology fair.

153
00:10:01,775 --> 00:10:06,517
Your turn. An A I that screens job
applications was trained only on past

154
00:10:06,517 --> 00:10:10,863
hires, who were mostly from one group.
What problem might it have?

155
00:10:11,313 --> 00:10:12,508
Pause the video now.

156
00:10:12,958 --> 00:10:17,699
Bias. It may learn to favor people like
the past hires, and unfairly reject

157
00:10:17,699 --> 00:10:18,142
others.

158
00:10:18,592 --> 00:10:21,579
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

159
00:10:22,729 --> 00:10:26,932
Let's sort real pieces of information, and
decide what's safe to post.

160
00:10:28,082 --> 00:10:33,186
The problem: you're making a public gaming
profile. For each item, is it PII, and

161
00:10:33,186 --> 00:10:34,951
should it go on the profile?

162
00:10:35,401 --> 00:10:39,445
Your favorite game, your full name, your
school's name, your birthday, and a

163
00:10:39,445 --> 00:10:39,924
nickname.

164
00:10:41,074 --> 00:10:45,362
Ask one question: could this help a
stranger find or identify me?

165
00:10:45,812 --> 00:10:51,036
Also remember that small pieces add up. A
school plus a birthday narrows things a

166
00:10:51,036 --> 00:10:51,294
lot.

167
00:10:52,444 --> 00:10:54,834
Favorite game: not PII. Fine to share.

168
00:10:55,284 --> 00:10:57,950
Full name: PII. Keep it off a public
profile.

169
00:10:58,400 --> 00:11:01,771
School: it reveals where you are every
day. Keep it private.

170
00:11:02,221 --> 00:11:07,611
Birthday: PII, and often used in security
questions. Keep it private. A made up

171
00:11:07,611 --> 00:11:08,770
nickname is fine.

172
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:13,867
The safe profile shows only your nickname
and your favorite game.

173
00:11:14,317 --> 00:11:19,637
Tip: check the app's privacy settings too.
Set your profile to friends only, and turn

174
00:11:19,637 --> 00:11:20,952
off location sharing.

175
00:11:22,102 --> 00:11:26,722
Your turn. Is a photo of you in front of
your house, with the house number showing,

176
00:11:26,722 --> 00:11:27,947
safe to post publicly?

177
00:11:28,397 --> 00:11:29,592
Pause the video now.

178
00:11:30,042 --> 00:11:34,122
No. Your face and your house number
together show who you are and where you

179
00:11:34,122 --> 00:11:34,394
live.

180
00:11:34,844 --> 00:11:36,828
Next, let's put the whole unit together.

181
00:11:37,977 --> 00:11:42,116
You've learned about passwords,
encryption, phishing, malware and privacy.

182
00:11:42,566 --> 00:11:46,982
Now let's set up one new account from
start to finish, using all of them.

183
00:11:48,132 --> 00:11:50,542
Here's the plan. You're joining a new
homework app.

184
00:11:50,992 --> 00:11:53,403
We'll make sure the account is safe at
every step.

185
00:11:54,553 --> 00:12:00,209
Step one: type the address yourself. Check
for https, so your data is encrypted on

186
00:12:00,209 --> 00:12:00,761
the way.

187
00:12:01,211 --> 00:12:04,987
And check the spelling. A fake site might
use a one instead of an L.

188
00:12:06,137 --> 00:12:09,892
Step two: the password. Maya twenty eleven
is short and guessable.

189
00:12:10,342 --> 00:12:15,429
Four random words is long, so it takes
ages to crack, and it's easy to remember.

190
00:12:15,429 --> 00:12:16,955
And it's used only here.

191
00:12:18,105 --> 00:12:20,793
Step three: turn on two factor
authentication.

192
00:12:21,243 --> 00:12:25,744
Now a stolen password isn't enough. An
attacker would also need your phone.

193
00:12:26,894 --> 00:12:31,972
Step four: a week later, an email asks you
to verify your password within an hour.

194
00:12:32,422 --> 00:12:37,369
Wrong sender, urgency, and a password
request: three red flags. Report it and

195
00:12:37,369 --> 00:12:38,011
delete it.

196
00:12:39,161 --> 00:12:44,263
Step five: privacy. Use a nickname, turn
off location sharing, and limit who can

197
00:12:44,263 --> 00:12:45,348
see your profile.

198
00:12:45,798 --> 00:12:47,867
Your account is now safe at every step.

199
00:12:49,017 --> 00:12:53,538
Your turn. Your password leaks in a data
breach, but two factor is on. Can an

200
00:12:53,538 --> 00:12:55,652
attacker log in? What should you do?

201
00:12:56,102 --> 00:12:57,296
Pause the video now.

202
00:12:57,746 --> 00:13:01,949
Not without your phone's code. But change
the password right away anyway.

203
00:13:02,399 --> 00:13:07,549
That's the end of Intro to Computer
Science. Well done! Next, try AP Computer

204
00:13:07,549 --> 00:13:08,820
Science Principles.

205
00:13:09,970 --> 00:13:14,399
If this helped, subscribe to School STEM,
Explained, so you don't miss the next

206
00:13:14,399 --> 00:13:14,792
lesson.

207
00:13:15,242 --> 00:13:16,074
See you there.

